1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,940
Hello everyone and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:05,600
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:09,000
My name is Catherine and today we're talking about weddings.

4
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:10,060
That's right.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:15,360
We are having a wedding but we're actually planning all the things out for the wedding.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:16,140
That's right.

7
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:22,340
So there are lots of things to think about when you're planning a wedding and I don't think that the woman in today's dialogue has forgotten anything.

8
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:26,260
Let's take a listen to the dialogue first and we'll be back in a moment.

9
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:36,160
Trina, will you marry me?

10
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,160
Yes, yes, yes!

11
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,400
Jared, of course I'll marry you.

12
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:43,550
Oh babe, I can't wait to spend the rest of my life with you.

13
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:48,050
I can't wait for all the adventures we're going to have, for all the fights and the laughter.

14
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,360
I can't wait to grow old and wrinkly with you.

15
00:00:51,390 --> 00:00:53,390
Oh Jared, I can't wait for our wedding.

16
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:57,790
I hope you don't mind but I've already chosen a date, six months from now in the summer.

17
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:03,200
Melissa saw you buying the ring last month so I had plenty of time to start planning.

18
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:03,430
She what?

19
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,410
Oh, don't worry sweetie.

20
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:07,800
I didn't know when you were going to propose.

21
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:08,970
It was still a nice surprise.

22
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:11,890
As I was saying, I've got it all planned out.

23
00:01:12,210 --> 00:01:13,430
There's almost nothing left to do.

24
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:17,530
I wrote up our guest list and we will have roughly 400 guests attending.

25
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:19,400
400?

26
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:21,580
No need to sweat it.

27
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,540
My parents agreed to pay for most of the wedding, which is going to be a low budget anyway.

28
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,240
So roughly 400 people, which means that the hall at Northwood Heights will be our reception venue.

29
00:01:30,820 --> 00:01:35,280
I thought it would be nice if we had the wedding at your parents' church, and my uncle of course would be officiating.

30
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,760
We'll meet with him soon for some pre-wedding counselling.

31
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,400
The music for the wedding ceremony was a no-brainer.

32
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,940
My stepsister and her string quartet will take care of that.

33
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:46,000
My cousin will be the official photographer.

34
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:49,150
I thought it would also be nice if his daughter could sing a solo.

35
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:52,010
Did you know that she's going to be a professional opera singer?

36
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:59,200
And then of course the ladies at the church would love to be our caterers for the banquet and will get the youth group to serve us.

37
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:05,040
I was thinking that your friend's band could be our entertainment for the night, though they might have to tone it down a bit.

38
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:06,150
Or we could hire a DJ.

39
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:10,430
Your sister's husband could get us a discount with that company that does the decor at weddings.

40
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:11,610
What's their name again?

41
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:19,080
I was thinking that we could have an island paradise themed wedding and our theme colour would be a soothing blue-like aquamarine.

42
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,680
And there would be a huge seashell on the wall behind the podium where we'll make our toasts.

43
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,240
What do you think of small packages of drink mixes for our wedding favours?

44
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:27,540
Who else am I missing?

45
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:32,200
Oh, your uncle could be our florist and his wife could make our wedding cake.

46
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:33,020
Oh wow.

47
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:35,320
See, it's going to be wonderful.

48
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:38,940
Oh, this wedding is going to be everything I ever dreamed of.

49
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:41,470
If I survive the next six months.

50
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:48,520
Alright we're back.

51
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,020
So she talked a lot.

52
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:53,440
So there are a lot of words and phrases here that we're going to take a look at.

53
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:58,920
So let's start with a couple of them on Language Takeaway.

54
00:02:58,920 --> 00:02:59,400
Language Takeaway.

55
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:05,000
Alright so on Language Takeaway, why don't we start off with the first word and the basic one.

56
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:09,280
When you plan a wedding, the guy needs to propose.

57
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:10,280
Or the woman.

58
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:12,160
Let's be 21st century here.

59
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:17,560
Normally it's the man but to propose in this instance means to propose marriage.

60
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:24,960
So basically this is the part where someone gets down on his knee and he says, you know, sweetie pie, I want to be with you forever.

61
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:25,640
Will you marry me?

62
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:26,960
Right.

63
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:27,960
To propose.

64
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:29,520
So to propose is to ask someone to marry you.

65
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:29,660
That's right.

66
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:31,520
But it can also mean something else, right?

67
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:32,360
To suggest.

68
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:33,220
That's right.

69
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:35,800
So I could propose an idea.

70
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:39,400
Or I could propose a plan.

71
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:45,840
But in this case, to propose has another meaning and it's basically to ask someone to marry you.

72
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:46,840
Okay.

73
00:03:46,840 --> 00:03:47,840
Very good.

74
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,160
So now we've proposed to someone.

75
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:56,390
So we need to start planning our wedding and I think one of the first steps is you need to know how many people are going to be there and who you're going to invite.

76
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:57,250
That's right.

77
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:04,090
So when you're planning a wedding, you have to think about the people that are going to attend the wedding so you prepare a guest list.

78
00:04:04,510 --> 00:04:04,810
Okay.

79
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:06,090
So a guest list.

80
00:04:06,330 --> 00:04:07,530
Now it's pretty simple, right?

81
00:04:07,730 --> 00:04:12,250
You have a list where you put everyone's name there and you know how many people are coming.

82
00:04:12,510 --> 00:04:12,710
Exactly.

83
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:14,800
Okay.

84
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:15,800
A guest list.

85
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,640
Now you have your guest list and now you need to pick a place where your wedding will be.

86
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:21,410
That's right.

87
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:24,120
So we often talk about two parts to a wedding.

88
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:27,110
The first part is the ceremony, the wedding ceremony.

89
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:30,110
Maybe if you're religious, that happens in a church.

90
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:31,720
Okay.

91
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:34,840
But there's a party afterwards and that's what we're talking about here.

92
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:35,930
We're talking about a reception.

93
00:04:36,510 --> 00:04:36,730
Okay.

94
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,840
A reception is basically the party part of the wedding where you drink and eat and listen to music and dance.

95
00:04:43,370 --> 00:04:49,030
So obviously for that, you need a space, a place to have the party and we call it the reception venue.

96
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:49,870
Okay.

97
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:50,470
A venue.

98
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:56,010
So as you said, the reception is the party and the venue is the place where you have it.

99
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:59,440
Now a venue is not a specific word, right?

100
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:02,350
You can use venue for many different places.

101
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:03,230
That's right.

102
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:07,570
So for example, someone's having a concert, I can ask him, hey, what's the venue?

103
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:08,330
Okay.

104
00:05:08,610 --> 00:05:13,080
That means what is the theater or where's this place you're going to have the concert?

105
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:13,540
Okay.

106
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:18,480
So for example, a stadium can be a venue or a restaurant can be a venue.

107
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:19,040
Exactly.

108
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:23,520
So a venue is a place where an event can be held.

109
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:24,520
Okay.

110
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:25,520
Very good.

111
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:27,040
So now we have our reception venue.

112
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:32,480
We have our guest list and the girl was talking about her uncle officiating.

113
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:34,320
What does she mean by to officiate?

114
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:34,960
Okay.

115
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:38,200
So like you said, the verb here is to officiate.

116
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,200
Her uncle will be officiating.

117
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:44,360
That means he will be the person who marries them.

118
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:53,040
So maybe you've seen movies before where there's a priest who says, you know, do you take this woman to be your lawfully wedded wife until death do you part?

119
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:59,680
That person who says that, the person who asks the questions, he or she officiates.

120
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:00,020
Okay.

121
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:01,680
Officiate.

122
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:07,480
So that's basically the action of marrying someone or leading the ceremony.

123
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:07,600
That's right.

124
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:10,380
And in America, this isn't just anybody.

125
00:06:11,160 --> 00:06:17,800
Someone has to have a license or has to be a priest and has to have the authority to officiate.

126
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:19,240
Okay.

127
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:20,240
Very good.

128
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:22,420
So that's a very interesting word there too, officiate.

129
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:31,120
Now moving on, when we talk about the food for the reception venue, well, we need somebody to cook it and prepare it and that's when we hire caterers.

130
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:32,100
That's right.

131
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:41,300
So a caterer is kind of like a cook, but a caterer basically arranges a meal for many people.

132
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:45,120
And usually the person will bring the food to you.

133
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:52,700
So for example, if we're going to have a party, Marco, and we're going to throw it at a venue 10 miles away, the caterer will bring the food to us.

134
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:53,200
Okay.

135
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,560
So that's a caterer.

136
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:58,640
You get someone to cater your wedding.

137
00:06:58,640 --> 00:06:59,640
That's right.

138
00:06:59,640 --> 00:06:59,810
Or cater a party.

139
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:04,270
So I've had parties before that I've called caterers to make the food for.

140
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:09,190
So basically they work with me and I tell them the kind of food I would like and they prepare it.

141
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:10,760
Okay.

142
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:11,760
Very good.

143
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:22,710
And now for our last part for the wedding planning, you have the food, you have the venue, you have everything, but you also need to decorate a little bit and this is when you call a florist.

144
00:07:23,250 --> 00:07:23,850
Florist.

145
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:28,680
So this sounds a lot like flower, right?

146
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:29,680
Florist flower.

147
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,930
And that's basically what this person does.

148
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:37,960
A florist is a person who prepares flowers or who cuts flowers and uses them to decorate.

149
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:38,100
Okay.

150
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:43,000
So you go to the florist to buy flowers, roses and different types of things.

151
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:43,900
Okay.

152
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:45,060
A florist.

153
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:46,800
Very good.

154
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,680
So we have six key words there that are related to planning a wedding.

155
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:57,600
Why don't we move on now to a couple of phrases on Fluency Builder.

156
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:57,880
Fluency Builder.

157
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:00,200
All right.

158
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:03,160
So this first phrase is a really good one.

159
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:04,680
It's a little bit gross.

160
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,020
We're talking about sweat.

161
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:10,740
The woman in this dialogue says, hey, no need to sweat it.

162
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:12,230
So what does this mean?

163
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:13,600
All right.

164
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:20,510
So if you say to sweat means when you're running, you're doing exercise, it's hot, water starts to come out of your skin.

165
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:21,410
Yummy.

166
00:08:22,290 --> 00:08:22,630
Right.

167
00:08:23,110 --> 00:08:29,930
But if the person says, hey, don't sweat it or no need to sweat it, it's a colloquial way of saying, don't worry.

168
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:30,850
Okay.

169
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:32,910
So we're not actually talking about sweat here.

170
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:37,480
So maybe Marco, you and I are working on a project together and I say, listen, Marco, I'm so sorry.

171
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:39,840
I have a family emergency.

172
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:41,480
I can't finish the project with you.

173
00:08:41,780 --> 00:08:43,640
Would you be able to help me out?

174
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:44,340
I'm so sorry.

175
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:44,900
Sure, sure.

176
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:45,800
Don't sweat it.

177
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:46,720
It's not a problem.

178
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:48,800
Okay.

179
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:49,800
So don't worry.

180
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:50,800
Don't worry.

181
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,800
Right.

182
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:57,040
So if you tell someone to not sweat it, don't sweat it, or no need to sweat it, you're basically telling them it's okay.

183
00:08:57,040 --> 00:08:57,620
It's not a problem.

184
00:08:58,040 --> 00:08:59,200
Everything's going to be fine.

185
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,200
Great.

186
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,940
So the next phrase here is also kind of relaxed.

187
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:03,760
It's kind of laid back.

188
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:09,240
So the woman is still describing some of the things she's prepared for the wedding and she says, hey, listen, don't worry.

189
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:11,100
The music was a no brainer.

190
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:12,920
Okay.

191
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:13,340
No brainer.

192
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:14,920
Okay.

193
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:15,920
This is really easy.

194
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:17,320
Well, your brain is inside your head, right?

195
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:24,960
So if you say no brainer, it's basically saying you don't have to think about it too much or it's very easy, very simple.

196
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:25,940
Okay.

197
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:33,580
So I was deciding on where to go on vacation and the options were Thailand or Antarctica.

198
00:09:34,060 --> 00:09:34,980
That's a no brainer.

199
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:36,560
Thailand.

200
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:38,400
Obviously, I want to go where it's sunny and warm.

201
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:42,380
So a no brainer means I didn't even have to think about it.

202
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:42,960
It was that easy.

203
00:09:43,380 --> 00:09:44,380
Right.

204
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:46,200
The decision making was a no brainer.

205
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:48,360
All right.

206
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:58,460
So for our next phrase, the girl was also talking about the music and she was talking about a band, maybe like a rock band and she said, well, maybe they could play, but they would need to tone it down.

207
00:09:59,060 --> 00:09:59,540
Okay.

208
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:04,040
So this is a favorite phrase of parents, I think, all over the English speaking world.

209
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:05,180
Tone it down.

210
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:07,460
In this case, we're talking about music.

211
00:10:07,820 --> 00:10:17,500
So maybe like you said, the friend's band is a rock and roll band and it's really loud and angry and in your face, but for a wedding, that's not appropriate.

212
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:22,620
So to tone it down means to make it more appropriate for the occasion.

213
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:28,340
So maybe less loud or no offensive language or no swear words.

214
00:10:28,620 --> 00:10:28,800
Right.

215
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:30,820
That's basically what tone it down means, right?

216
00:10:30,940 --> 00:10:35,460
If someone says you need to tone it down, it's a little bit more direct.

217
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:44,240
Like you said, if your parents tell you this, it's maybe because you're being a little bit too loud or you're yelling or you're being irrespectful.

218
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:47,700
So that's why your parents would tell you, hey, you need to tone it down a bit.

219
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:48,260
That's right.

220
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:54,800
Or sometimes we have this in the office where someone doesn't understand how to communicate in a positive way.

221
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:58,060
And so they say, oh, that paper you did was really bad.

222
00:10:58,240 --> 00:10:59,240
It was stupid.

223
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:00,060
I don't ever want to see it again.

224
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:03,240
Say, well, maybe you need to tone it down a little bit.

225
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,800
Think about the words you're using and make them a little bit less harsh.

226
00:11:07,840 --> 00:11:16,520
So kind of to relax, to reduce your level of being aggressive, being loud, that's to tone it down.

227
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:16,960
Exactly.

228
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:18,840
Okay.

229
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:19,240
A lot of great phrases there.

230
00:11:19,840 --> 00:11:24,480
Why don't we now go back, listen to our great dialogue one last time and we'll be back to talk a little bit more.

231
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:35,240
Trina, will you marry me?

232
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:36,780
Yes, yes, yes.

233
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:38,860
Jared, of course I'll marry you.

234
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:42,450
Oh babe, I can't wait to spend the rest of my life with you.

235
00:11:42,710 --> 00:11:46,950
I can't wait for all the adventures we're going to have, for all the fights and the laughter.

236
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:50,280
I can't wait to grow old and wrinkly with you.

237
00:11:50,290 --> 00:11:52,270
Oh Jared, I can't wait for our wedding.

238
00:11:52,650 --> 00:11:56,670
I hope you don't mind, but I've already chosen a date six months from now in the summer.

239
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:02,080
Melissa saw you buying the ring last month, so I had plenty of time to start planning.

240
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:02,310
She what?

241
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:06,090
Oh don't worry sweetie, I didn't know when you were going to propose.

242
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:07,870
It was still a nice surprise.

243
00:12:08,630 --> 00:12:10,770
As I was saying, I've got it all planned out.

244
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:12,310
There's almost nothing left to do.

245
00:12:12,750 --> 00:12:16,430
I wrote up our guest list and we will have roughly 400 guests attending.

246
00:12:18,260 --> 00:12:18,400
400?

247
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:20,480
No need to sweat it.

248
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:24,420
My parents agreed to pay for most of the wedding, which is going to be a low budget anyway.

249
00:12:25,060 --> 00:12:29,140
So roughly 400 people, which means that the hall at Northwood Heights will be our reception venue.

250
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:34,120
I thought it would be nice if we had the wedding at your parents' church, and my uncle of course would be officiating.

251
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:37,640
We'll meet with him soon for some pre-wedding counselling.

252
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:40,280
The music for the wedding ceremony was a no-brainer.

253
00:12:40,280 --> 00:12:42,840
My stepsister and her string quartet will take care of that.

254
00:12:43,420 --> 00:12:44,920
My cousin will be the official photographer.

255
00:12:45,510 --> 00:12:48,030
I thought it would also be nice if his daughter could sing a solo.

256
00:12:48,650 --> 00:12:50,890
Did you know that she's going to be a professional opera singer?

257
00:12:52,640 --> 00:12:58,080
And then of course the ladies at the church would love to be our caterers for the banquet and will get the youth group to serve us.

258
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:04,280
I was thinking that your friend's band could be our entertainment for the night, though they might have to turn it down a bit.

259
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:05,030
Or we could hire a DJ.

260
00:13:05,680 --> 00:13:09,310
Your sister's husband could get us a discount with that company that does the decor at weddings.

261
00:13:09,910 --> 00:13:10,470
What's their name again?

262
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:17,280
I was thinking that we could have an island paradise themed wedding, and our theme colour would be a soothing blue-like aquamarine.

263
00:13:17,980 --> 00:13:22,200
And there will be a huge seashell on the wall behind the podium where we'll make our toasts.

264
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:25,120
What do you think of small packages of drink mixes for our wedding favours?

265
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:26,440
Who else am I missing?

266
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:31,160
Oh, your uncle could be our florist, and his wife could make our wedding cake.

267
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:31,940
Oh wow.

268
00:13:32,440 --> 00:13:33,800
See, it's going to be wonderful.

269
00:13:34,820 --> 00:13:37,840
Oh, this wedding is going to be everything I ever dreamed of.

270
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:40,340
If I survive the next six months.

271
00:13:48,510 --> 00:13:53,770
Alright so about weddings and planning out a wedding, do you think 400 guests is a small wedding?

272
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:55,840
No.

273
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:57,800
No, this woman is out of control.

274
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:06,900
In fact, in English, we have a word for a bride, or like a woman who's getting married, that describes someone who's completely nuts about the wedding.

275
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:08,080
We call her a bridezilla.

276
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:08,480
Bridezilla.

277
00:14:09,080 --> 00:14:10,080
Like Godzilla.

278
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:11,080
Exactly, the movie Godzilla.

279
00:14:11,080 --> 00:14:12,080
He's a monster.

280
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:13,080
And he's out of control.

281
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:13,900
He's out of control.

282
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:20,960
And a bride who's so obsessed with her wedding that nothing else is important, she is a bridezilla.

283
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:26,600
Now it's a very big thing for a woman, right, for her wedding to be nice and perfect and everything.

284
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:28,560
Do you think guys are more relaxed in this sense?

285
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:29,480
I don't know.

286
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:31,520
I think it depends on the guy, as always.

287
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:35,360
There are some people who want to be very involved, and some people who don't care.

288
00:14:36,280 --> 00:14:40,400
But in general, I think men are less worried about the details.

289
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:43,380
Just as long as there's a wedding, it's fine.

290
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:45,040
Exactly.

291
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:48,360
Although a lot of men, when the wedding actually happens, start to get very nervous.

292
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:51,400
But kind of like having doubts, maybe cold feet.

293
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:52,720
Yes, cold feet.

294
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:54,160
Like should I get married?

295
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:55,180
Should I not get married?

296
00:14:56,430 --> 00:14:59,370
I think it's very interesting because you see this in movies a lot.

297
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:10,590
And I've always wondered how often or how common is it for a guy or a girl to say no or to leave a wedding when it has already started.

298
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:18,840
Because you know, you have invited all these people, all these friends and family, maybe people came from different countries, and food is paid for, venue.

299
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,960
You spend so much money on a wedding and for you to say no.

300
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:23,130
I don't know how common it is.

301
00:15:23,210 --> 00:15:28,450
It's very common in movies, but I really don't know anyone who's experienced this.

302
00:15:28,690 --> 00:15:30,410
I don't either, actually, now that you bring it up.

303
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:32,360
So maybe we could talk about this.

304
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:33,490
Is this common in your country?

305
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:36,030
First of all, do people spend a lot of money on weddings?

306
00:15:36,630 --> 00:15:42,050
And second of all, have you ever heard of someone actually leaving the wedding before they finished?

307
00:15:42,440 --> 00:15:44,160
Yes, during the ceremony.

308
00:15:44,160 --> 00:15:44,890
Not before, during.

309
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:46,160
During the ceremony.

310
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:48,280
So our website is EnglishPod.com.

311
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:49,480
And we hope to see you there.

312
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:50,480
Until next time.

313
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:58,537
Bye.
